WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.380
Now let’s look at the four jobs of a manager.

2
00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:12.287
This picture is based on Gary Yukl’s research on what effective managers do, used here as a guide.

3
00:00:12.337 --> 00:00:18.660
Every manager, in every part of Vanderbilt, does the same four jobs every week.

4
00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:26.808
The first job is getting the work done: plan it, say what good looks like, check it before it is due,

5
00:00:26.858 --> 00:00:29.470
and fix the cause when it breaks.

6
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:36.247
The second is taking care of your people: listen, grow them, thank them, trust them.

7
00:00:36.297 --> 00:00:44.395
The third is making things better: explain the why, describe where the team is going, let people try,

8
00:00:44.445 --> 00:00:53.270
look back. The fourth is connecting your team to the rest of Vanderbilt: know the people your team depends on,

9
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:57.223
watch for what is coming, speak up for your team.

10
00:00:57.273 --> 00:01:05.936
Nothing here is new work. It is the same work, sorted, and the assessment you took scores these same habits.

11
00:01:05.986 --> 00:01:10.211
The skill is noticing which job this week needs most.

12
00:01:10.261 --> 00:01:15.536
For example: a facilities supervisor starts Monday with four asks.

13
00:01:15.586 --> 00:01:21.425
A backlog of work orders. A new technician who wants to learn the boiler.

14
00:01:21.475 --> 00:01:26.750
A new work-order system. And Events asking for his crew on Friday.

15
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.881
One list, four different jobs. Sorting it tells him where the week is heavy.

16
00:01:32.931 --> 00:01:36.834
This week: sort your own list into the four jobs.

